WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.024
Galaxy kinematics modeling is the computational
bottleneck when astronomers try to jointly

00:00:06.104 --> 00:00:10.631
model gravitational lensing and kinematics to
measure the Hubble constant.

00:00:10.711 --> 00:00:14.803
This paper introduces SKiNN, the Stellar Kinematics
Neural Network,

00:00:14.883 --> 00:00:21.839
a proof-of-concept emulator that reproduces slow
physics-based kinematics calculations at a fraction of the cost.

00:00:21.919 --> 00:00:23.831
After a one-time training phase,

00:00:23.911 --> 00:00:29.311
SKiNN generates velocity dispersion images that are
accurate to within about one percent

00:00:29.391 --> 00:00:31.739
in the scientifically important region,

00:00:31.819 --> 00:00:36.471
while running two to three orders of magnitude
faster than existing methods.

00:00:36.551 --> 00:00:43.196
This speedup finally makes it feasible to model
spatially resolved kinematic data jointly with lensing data,

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:47.990
addressing the largest source of uncertainty in
Hubble constant measurements.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:54.095
Gravitational lensing measures the Hubble constant
by comparing time delays between multiple

00:00:54.175 --> 00:00:58.133
images of a distant source to a model of the lens
galaxy's mass.

00:00:58.213 --> 00:01:04.000
But lensing degeneracies allow many different mass
distributions to reproduce the same image,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.912
so independent kinematic constraints are needed to
break them.

00:01:07.992 --> 00:01:09.237
The trouble is speed.

00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:13.275
Modeling galaxy kinematics with a physics code like
JAM is slow,

00:01:13.355 --> 00:01:20.089
and it must be recomputed for every single
likelihood evaluation inside a Markov Chain Monte Carlo sampling.

00:01:20.169 --> 00:01:26.714
This makes jointly exploring the full lensing and
kinematics parameter space computationally prohibitive,

00:01:26.794 --> 00:01:31.320
forcing modelers to cut corners by fitting the two
components separately.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.663
Recently,

00:01:35.743 --> 00:01:39.705
spatially resolved kinematics of lensing galaxies
became available through instruments

00:01:39.785 --> 00:01:43.748
like the James Webb Space Telescope.

00:01:43.828 --> 00:01:47.791
Traditional spherical Jeans models are too
simplistic for this richer data,

00:01:47.871 --> 00:01:51.834
and they lack self-consistency with the elliptical
mass models used in lensing.

00:01:51.914 --> 00:01:55.876
The natural next step is axisymmetric modeling with
software such as JAM,

00:01:55.956 --> 00:01:59.919
but JAM is expensive.

00:01:59.999 --> 00:02:03.962
Existing frameworks that combine it with lens
modeling either fit the two separately or

00:02:04.042 --> 00:02:08.005
remain far too slow for full joint parameter exploration.

00:02:08.085 --> 00:02:12.047
The key insight of this work is that JAM's slow
physics can be emulated by a neural network,

00:02:12.127 --> 00:02:16.090
keeping the physics of the overall model while
removing the computational bottleneck.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.988
The core contribution is SKiNN, the Stellar
Kinematics Neural Network.

00:02:21.068 --> 00:02:25.586
It is the first time the strategy of replacing an
expensive model component with a neural

00:02:25.666 --> 00:02:30.183
network has been applied to the kinematic-modeling
aspect of gravitational lens modeling.

00:02:30.263 --> 00:02:34.781
Crucially,

00:02:34.861 --> 00:02:39.379
SKiNN emulates only the JAM physics calculation
rather than being applied directly to observations.

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:43.977
This design choice keeps the physics of the overall
model intact while exploiting the

00:02:44.057 --> 00:02:48.574
speed and versatility of neural networks.

00:02:48.654 --> 00:02:53.172
The result is a roughly 300-fold speedup at
sub-percent accuracy,

00:02:53.252 --> 00:02:57.770
which finally makes joint lensing plus kinematics
modeling computationally feasible.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:02.792
SKiNN precalculates JAM for use in lensing applications.

00:03:02.872 --> 00:03:07.514
Formally,

00:03:07.594 --> 00:03:12.235
it is a function that maps an eight-dimensional
vector of galaxy parameters into a d by d image of vrms,

00:03:12.315 --> 00:03:16.957
the quadratic sum of velocity dispersion and
rotational velocity.

00:03:17.037 --> 00:03:21.679
The eight inputs describe a power-law elliptical
mass distribution and an elliptical Sérsic light profile,

00:03:21.759 --> 00:03:26.401
plus the galaxy inclination and orbital anisotropy.

00:03:26.481 --> 00:03:31.123
The architecture consists of five blocks,

00:03:31.203 --> 00:03:35.845
each with two two-dimensional convolutional layers
followed by an upsampling layer and a ReLU nonlinearity,

00:03:35.925 --> 00:03:40.566
for about seven million trainable parameters.

00:03:40.646 --> 00:03:45.288
It is trained by minimizing a standard
mean-squared-error loss with the Adam optimizer,

00:03:45.368 --> 00:03:50.010
using a dataset generated by JAM through the GLEE
software so the network learns to mimic the JAM procedure.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.968
The training set is constructed entirely with JAM,

00:03:54.048 --> 00:03:57.114
the physics code SKiNN is meant to imitate.

00:03:57.194 --> 00:04:00.992
The authors created five thousand input-output pairs,

00:04:01.072 --> 00:04:05.528
using four thousand for training, five hundred for
validation,

00:04:05.608 --> 00:04:08.309
and five hundred held out for testing.

00:04:08.389 --> 00:04:11.309
Each velocity image is 551 by 551 pixels,

00:04:11.389 --> 00:04:14.674
generated at higher resolution than real data.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:19.137
Creating a single image with JAM takes about
fifteen seconds,

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:22.503
which underscores why emulation is worthwhile.

00:04:22.583 --> 00:04:29.600
The network is implemented in PyTorch with PyTorch
Lightning and trained on five Tesla P100 GPUs,

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.990
each with sixteen gigabytes of memory, over roughly
one day.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:39.152
Once trained,

00:04:39.232 --> 00:04:44.014
SKiNN produces a velocity image from an input
parameter vector in about fifty milliseconds on a single GPU,

00:04:44.094 --> 00:04:48.876
which is roughly three hundred times faster than JAM.

00:04:48.956 --> 00:04:53.738
Accuracy is assessed on the five hundred held-out
test images by computing the relative error at each pixel.

00:04:53.818 --> 00:04:58.600
While some outer regions can show errors of a few percent,

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.462
the important innermost region, where real data
actually constrains the model,

00:05:03.542 --> 00:05:08.324
is matched to within plus or minus one percent for
almost all pixels.

00:05:08.404 --> 00:05:13.186
Averaged over entire images,

00:05:13.266 --> 00:05:18.048
the median absolute error is about 0.47 percent and
the ninetieth percentile is about 1.1 percent.

00:05:18.128 --> 00:05:22.910
Both are comfortably below the typical
two-percent-or-greater systematic uncertainty in real velocity measurements.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:26.869
As a proof-of-concept workshop paper,

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:30.528
this work does not present a formal ablation study.

00:05:30.608 --> 00:05:34.187
Instead,

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:37.846
performance is characterized by the distribution of
per-pixel errors across the five hundred test images,

00:05:37.926 --> 00:05:41.504
and by comparing inference on GPU versus CPU,

00:05:41.584 --> 00:05:45.163
where the GPU is about ten times faster.

00:05:45.243 --> 00:05:48.822
The analysis also distinguishes the innermost
two-arcsecond region,

00:05:48.902 --> 00:05:52.481
where errors stay within one percent, from the
outer parts of the image,

00:05:52.561 --> 00:05:56.140
where errors can rise to a few percent but the data
provides little constraint anyway.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.516
Here are the headline numbers.

00:05:58.596 --> 00:06:02.184
SKiNN runs about three hundred times faster than JAM,

00:06:02.264 --> 00:06:08.759
taking roughly fifty milliseconds per image on a
GPU compared to about fifteen seconds for JAM.

00:06:08.839 --> 00:06:13.811
Its error is under one percent within the innermost
two-arcsecond region,

00:06:13.891 --> 00:06:19.901
with a median absolute error around 0.47 percent
and a ninetieth percentile of about 1.1

00:06:19.981 --> 00:06:22.670
percent across five hundred test images.

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:28.760
The network maps an eight-dimensional parameter
vector to a velocity image and has about

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.182
seven million trainable parameters.

00:06:31.262 --> 00:06:33.951
It was trained on five thousand samples,

00:06:34.031 --> 00:06:39.626
split four thousand for training and five hundred
each for validation and testing,

00:06:39.706 --> 00:06:42.740
over roughly one day on five Tesla P100 GPUs.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.077
The takeaway is simple.

00:06:47.157 --> 00:06:51.114
By training a neural network to emulate the slow
kinematics physics of JAM,

00:06:51.194 --> 00:06:55.151
SKiNN achieves roughly a three-hundred-fold speedup
at sub-percent accuracy in the region that matters.

00:06:55.231 --> 00:06:59.188
This removes the main computational bottleneck and
makes it feasible to jointly model

00:06:59.268 --> 00:07:03.225
gravitational lensing together with spatially
resolved kinematics,

00:07:03.305 --> 00:07:07.262
which corrects for the largest source of
uncertainty in measuring the Hubble constant.

00:07:07.342 --> 00:07:11.299
More broadly,

00:07:11.379 --> 00:07:15.336
the strategy of swapping an expensive but
calculable model piece for a trained neural network,

00:07:15.416 --> 00:07:19.373
while keeping the surrounding physics intact,

00:07:19.453 --> 00:07:23.410
likely transfers to many other scientific modeling
problems.
